      "name": "ConvertFloatToInt16Stereo",
      "signature": "void ConvertFloatToInt16Stereo(short * pnOutputBuffer, float * pflInputSamples, float flScaleFactor, uint dwSamplePairs)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Converts interleaved 32-bit float stereo audio samples to 16-bit signed integers with scaling and saturation clamping.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize loop counter to half the sample pair count (dwSamplePairs >> 1)\n2. Begin main conversion loop processing stereo sample pairs\n3. Load left channel float sample from input buffer (pflInputSamples[0])\n4. Load right channel float sample from input buffer (pflInputSamples[2])\n5. Multiply both samples by scale factor (flScaleFactor) \n6. Round both scaled samples to nearest integers using ROUND()\n7. Check left sample for saturation: clamp to [-32768, 32767] range\n8. Check right sample for saturation: clamp to [-32768, 32767] range\n9. Store clamped 16-bit samples to output buffer as short values\n10. Advance input pointer by 4 floats (2 stereo pairs = L,R,L,R)\n11. Advance output pointer by 2 shorts (1 stereo pair = L,R)\n12. Decrement loop counter and repeat until all pairs processed\n\nParameters:\npnOutputBuffer (short *): Output buffer for 16-bit signed stereo samples\npflInputSamples (float *): Input buffer containing 32-bit float stereo samples\nflScaleFactor (float): Scaling factor applied to input samples before conversion\ndwSamplePairs (uint): Number of stereo sample pairs to process\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value, results written directly to output buffer\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Left sample >= 32768 (0x8000): Clamp to 32767 (0x7fff)\n- Left sample <= -32768 (-0x8000): Clamp to -32768 (0x8000)\n- Right sample >= 32768: Clamp to 32767 (0x7fff)\n- Right sample <= -32768: Clamp to -32768 (0x8000)\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x8000 (32768): Maximum positive value before saturation\n0x7fff (32767): Maximum positive 16-bit signed integer\n-0x8000 (-32768): Minimum negative 16-bit signed integer\n\nError Handling:\n- Saturation protection prevents integer overflow during float-to-int conversion\n- Input samples outside 16-bit range are automatically clamped to valid bounds\n- No error return codes - conversion always succeeds with clamping\n\nNote: Function uses 2 phantom stack variables (local_8, local_c) for FPU operations optimized away by decompiler.",
