      "name": "ProcessColorChannelsMMX",
      "signature": "void ProcessColorChannelsMMX(uint dwPixelCount)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Performs MMX-optimized color channel processing for video/image data.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load global MMX constants: zero mask, Y scale factor, UV scale factor, bias values\n2. Calculate source buffer end address: base + (dwPixelCount * 16 bytes)\n3. Process first buffer loop:\n   a. Load two consecutive 32-bit pixels from destination buffer\n   b. Unpack pixels to 16-bit components using zero extension (PUNPCKLBW)\n   c. Subtract zero mask bias for unsigned saturation (PSUBUSW)  \n   d. Apply high-precision scaling using multiply-high (PMULHW)\n   e. Add bias value with signed saturation (PADDSW)\n   f. Subtract bias to clamp negative values (PSUBUSW)\n   g. Apply component scaling factors using multiplication (PMULLW)\n   h. Duplicate components and store as 64-bit values in source buffer\n   i. Advance pointers: destination +8 bytes, source +32 bytes\n   j. Continue until source pointer reaches calculated end\n4. Process second buffer loop (identical algorithm):\n   a. Uses g_pDstBufferEnd and g_pSrcBufferEnd pointers\n   b. Processes remaining pixels with same MMX operations\n   c. Updates global end pointers after completion\n5. Restore MMX state and return\n\nParameters:\ndwPixelCount - Number of pixel quads to process (multiply by 4 for actual pixel count)\n\nReturns:\nvoid\n\nSpecial Cases:\nBuffer bounds are checked through pointer comparison rather than counter\nMMX state is implicitly cleared by subsequent EMMS instruction (not in this function)\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x4 (SHL) - Convert pixel count to 16-byte stride (4 pixels * 4 bytes = 16)\n0x8 - Destination buffer advancement (2 pixels * 4 bytes)\n0x20 - Source buffer advancement (8 pixels * 4 bytes)\n\nMemory Layout:\nGlobal constants at 0x1002dfe0, 0x1002dfd8, 0x1002dfd0, 0x1002dfb8\nSource/destination buffer pointers at 0x100332e0-0x100332ec\nEach pixel is 32-bit DWORD with RGBA components\nOutput buffer stores duplicated 16-bit components",
