      "name": "ProcessPixelBufferMmxBlend",
      "signature": "void ProcessPixelBufferMmxBlend(int nPixelCount)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Processes pixel data using MMX-optimized blending operations on two buffer regions.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load MMX constants for zero mask, multiplier, and rounding values\n2. Calculate source buffer end position based on pixel count parameter\n3. FIRST LOOP - Process source buffer from current position to calculated end:\n   a. Load two consecutive 32-bit pixel values from destination buffer\n   b. Unpack pixels to 16-bit components using MMX zero mask subtraction\n   c. Left shift components by 2 bits for precision scaling\n   d. Apply high multiplication using MMX multiplier constant\n   e. Add rounding constant for proper numerical rounding\n   f. Subtract rounding constant to implement saturation\n   g. Extract 16-bit components and perform cross-multiplication with pack mask\n   h. Pack results back to 32-bit values and store to source buffer\n   i. Advance both source and destination buffer pointers by 64 bytes\n4. SECOND LOOP - Process remaining buffer region from end position to final end:\n   a. Repeat same MMX operations as first loop\n   b. Process pixel data with same blend algorithm\n   c. Store results and advance pointers\n5. Update global source buffer end pointer\n\nParameters:\nnPixelCount - Number of pixels to process (determines buffer range)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Results stored directly to global source buffer memory\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Buffer pointers are incremented by 64 bytes (16 pixels) per iteration\n- Uses MMX packed 64-bit operations for SIMD performance\n- Implements saturated arithmetic to prevent overflow\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x02 - Left shift amount for precision scaling\n0x10 - Buffer advance increment (64 bytes = 16 pixels)\n0x8 - Pixel count scaling factor for address calculation\n\nStructure Layout:\nNo structures accessed - operates on raw pixel buffer data",
