      "name": "ProcessHuffmanDecodedSignals",
      "signature": "uint ProcessHuffmanDecodedSignals(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "uint",
      "comment": "Processes Huffman-decoded audio signals with frequency domain transformations and windowing.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize stack allocation for signal processing buffers (0xa084 bytes)\n2. Extract sample count and bitstream position from input parameters\n3. Decode initial frequency values using floating-point conversion (FUN_1001a3f0)\n4. Initialize bit position tracker (6 bits available) and bitstream pointer\n5. Process windowing scale factors if channel count > 0:\n   - Extract 8-bit scale indices from bitstream using variable bit extraction\n   - Generate floating-point scale values via FUN_1001d060()\n   - Store scale factors in windowing array starting at stack offset 0x20\n6. Perform Huffman decoding loop for sample indices 2 to nSampleCount:\n   - Extract single bit flag to determine code type (fixed vs variable length)\n   - For variable length codes: use 4-bit index into g_abHuffmanCodeLengths lookup table\n   - Decode run-length value: multiply table entry by 8 and add to current block index\n   - Extract decoded sample values using variable bit-width extraction (1-32 bits)\n   - Handle zero run-length encoding: fill sample buffer with zeros using REP STOSD\n   - For non-zero samples: extract sign bit and apply two's complement if negative\n   - Store decoded samples as 16-bit signed values in sample buffer\n7. Apply windowing scaling to decoded samples if channel count > 0:\n   - Iterate through each channel using channel size array (pdwChannelSizes)\n   - Multiply sample pairs by corresponding scale factors from windowing array\n   - Convert 16-bit samples to floating-point and apply multiplicative scaling\n   - Store results in frequency domain output buffer starting at stack offset 0x209c\n8. Perform frequency domain reordering (mirroring/conjugation):\n   - Copy first half of spectrum to second half with sign inversion on imaginary parts\n   - Implements complex conjugate symmetry for real-valued time domain signals\n9. Process final signal arrays using external function ProcessSignalArrays()\n10. Apply quantization table processing via FUN_1001a430()\n11. Return updated bitstream position with bounds checking (0x1fffffff mask)\n\nParameters:\nnSampleCount (uint): Number of audio samples to process (passed via stack offset 0xa088)\nflFreqStep (float): Frequency step size for spectrum processing (stack offset 0xa08c)\npInputBuffer (int *): Input audio buffer pointer (stack offset 0xa090)\npOutputBuffer (float *): Output processed audio buffer (stack offset 0xa094)\npwQuantTable (ushort *): Quantization table for audio encoding (stack offset 0xa098)\npdwBitstream (uint *): Compressed bitstream data pointer (stack offset 0xa09c)\nnChannelCount (int): Number of audio channels to process (stack offset 0xa0a0)\npdwChannelSizes (uint *): Array of sample counts per channel (stack offset 0xa0a4)\n\nReturns:\nuint: Updated bitstream position with 29-bit bounds masking (0x1fffffff)\n      Calculation: (bitstream_base * 0x1fffffff + current_position) & 0x1fffffff\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Zero channel count: skips windowing scale processing (jumps to huffman_decode_loop)\n- Zero run-length codes: fills sample buffer regions with zeros using optimized REP STOSD\n- Bit buffer exhaustion: automatically refills from next bitstream word\n- Sample count clipping: ensures block indices never exceed nSampleCount limit\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0xa084 (41092): Total stack allocation size for processing buffers\n0x1fffffff (536870911): 29-bit mask for bitstream position bounds checking  \n0x3ffffff (67108863): 26-bit mask for bitstream data extraction\n0x1f (31): 5-bit mask for bit shift operations (prevents undefined behavior)\n0x20 (32): Stack offset for windowing scale factor array\n0x2084 (8324): Stack offset for frequency domain output buffer  \n0x209c (8348): Stack offset for final processed output array\n6: Initial bit position in compressed bitstream\n8: Fixed increment for simple Huffman codes (non-variable length)\n\nStructure Layout:\nOffset | Size | Field Name | Type | Description\n-------|------|------------|------|-------------\n0x20   | 128  | ScaleArray | float[32] | Windowing scale factors per channel\n0x84   | 8192 | SampleBuffer | short[4096] | Decoded Huffman samples (16-bit)\n0x2084 | 2048 | FreqBuffer | float[512] | Frequency domain output buffer\n0x209c | 2048 | OutputBuffer | float[512] | Final processed output array\n\nHuffman Decoding State Machine:\nState 1: Single bit flag extraction \u2192 determines code type (fixed=0, variable=1)  \nState 2A: Fixed length code \u2192 add 8 to current block index\nState 2B: Variable length code \u2192 extract 4-bit table index, lookup run-length multiplier\nState 3: Block bounds checking \u2192 clamp to nSampleCount maximum  \nState 4: Sample bit-width extraction \u2192 decode 1-32 bit values per table specification\nState 5A: Zero run-length \u2192 fill buffer region with zeros (REP STOSD optimization)\nState 5B: Non-zero samples \u2192 extract samples with sign bit processing\nState 6: Sign bit application \u2192 negate sample if sign bit = 1\nState 7: Loop continuation \u2192 advance to next block until all samples decoded",
