      "name": "ProcessFftBitReversalAndButterfly",
      "signature": "void ProcessFftBitReversalAndButterfly(int nLength, int * pBitReversalTable, float * pComplexData)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Performs FFT bit-reversal preprocessing and butterfly operations for Fast Fourier Transform.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize bit-reversal table and zero the first entry\n2. Generate bit-reversal indices for reordering complex data\n3. Perform iterative radix-2 FFT decomposition with stage doubling\n4. Negate DC component imaginary part (frequency bin 0)\n5. Execute butterfly operations in two phases based on stage alignment\n6. Phase A: Standard butterfly with dual complex pointer processing\n7. Phase B: Alternate butterfly path for misaligned stage boundaries\n8. Apply complex conjugation (negate imaginary parts) during butterfly swaps\n9. Process all frequency bins with bit-reversed addressing\n\nParameters:\nnLength - Size of input data array (must be power of 2, minimum 8)\npBitReversalTable - Pointer to integer array for bit-reversed indices  \npComplexData - Pointer to interleaved complex float array (real, imag, real, imag...)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Function modifies pComplexData in-place and populates pBitReversalTable\n\nSpecial Cases:\nInput length <= 4: Skips bit-reversal table generation\nStage alignment: Two butterfly processing paths based on 4*stage vs length comparison\nDC component: Always negates imaginary part at pComplexData[1]\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x4 (4) - Minimum FFT size threshold for bit-reversal processing\n0x1 (1) - Initial stage size for radix-2 decomposition  \n0x2 (2) - Stage doubling factor for iterative FFT\n0x8 (8) - Byte stride for complex number pairs (2 floats * 4 bytes each)\n\nError Handling:\nNo explicit validation - assumes valid power-of-2 input length\nBuffer overrun protection relies on caller providing correct array sizes\nUndefined behavior if nLength not power of 2 or arrays too small\n\nFFT Algorithm Details:\nUses Cooley-Tukey radix-2 decimation-in-frequency algorithm\nBit-reversal table maps natural order indices to bit-reversed positions\nButterfly operations perform complex multiplication with twiddle factors\nConjugation during swap implements frequency domain transformation\nStage-by-stage processing maintains numerical stability",
