      "comment": "Calculates and fills bundle size array for different compression parameters.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate size for 4-bit data with base size (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize, 4, 1)\n2. Calculate size for 4-bit data with half base size (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize>>1, 4, 1)  \n3. Calculate size for 8-bit data with 0x40 multiplier (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize, 8, 0x40)\n4. Calculate size for 8-bit data with 8 multiplier (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize, 8, 8)\n5. Calculate size for 5-bit data with base size (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize, 5, 1)\n6. Calculate duplicate 5-bit size (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize, 5, 1)\n7. Calculate size for 11-bit data with base size (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize, 0xb, 1)\n8. Calculate duplicate 11-bit size (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize, 0xb, 1)\n9. Calculate size for 4-bit data with 0x30 multiplier (param: 0x200, dwBaseSize, 4, 0x30)\n\nParameters:\npBundleSizes (uint *): Pointer to array of 9 uint elements for storing calculated sizes\ndwBaseSize (uint): Base size parameter used in bundle size calculations\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Results stored in pBundleSizes array\n\nSpecial Cases:\nStep 2 uses right-shift (dwBaseSize >> 1) to halve the base size\nSteps 5-6 and 7-8 calculate identical values (possible redundancy)\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x200 (512): Fixed first parameter to calculation function\n0x40 (64): High multiplier for 8-bit compression  \n0x30 (48): High multiplier for 4-bit compression\n0xb (11): Bit depth for high-precision compression\n\nArray Layout:\nIndex 0: 4-bit base size\nIndex 1: 4-bit half size  \nIndex 2: 8-bit with 0x40 multiplier\nIndex 3: 8-bit with 8 multiplier\nIndex 4: 5-bit base size\nIndex 5: 5-bit base size (duplicate)\nIndex 6: 11-bit base size\nIndex 7: 11-bit base size (duplicate)\nIndex 8: 4-bit with 0x30 multiplier",
