      "name": "ProcessDataThroughLookupTable",
      "signature": "int ProcessDataThroughLookupTable(int nElementCount)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "int",
      "comment": "Processes data through a lookup table, converting byte sequences to 32-bit integer arrays.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate destination buffer size by multiplying element count by 3 (nElementCount * 3)\n2. Update global source buffer pointer (g_pSrcBufferCurrent) to new end position\n3. Process first buffer region (Current to End):\n   a. Read 4 consecutive bytes from destination buffer at current position\n   b. Look up each byte in global lookup table (g_anLookupTable) to get 32-bit values\n   c. Combine lookup results using bit shifting and OR operations:\n      - Byte 0: Direct value OR (Byte 1 lookup << 24)\n      - Byte 1: (Byte 1 lookup >> 8) OR (Byte 2 lookup << 16)  \n      - Byte 2: (Byte 2 lookup >> 16) OR (Byte 3 lookup << 8)\n   d. Store three 32-bit results in source buffer and advance pointers\n   e. Repeat until source buffer current reaches calculated end position\n4. Update global source buffer end pointer (g_pSrcBufferEnd) \n5. Repeat identical process for second buffer region (End buffer):\n   a. Process bytes from g_pDstBufferEnd using same lookup and bit manipulation\n   b. Continue until all elements processed\n6. Update global destination buffer end pointer (g_pDstBufferEnd)\n7. Return 0 (success indicator)\n\nParameters:\nnElementCount - Number of data elements to process through lookup transformation\n\nReturns:\n0 - Always returns success (no error conditions detected)\n\nSpecial Cases:\nUses global buffer management system with separate Current/End buffer pairs\nPerforms identical processing logic on two different buffer regions\nEach input element generates exactly 3 output 32-bit values\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x3 (3) - Output expansion ratio (1 element -> 3 32-bit values)\n0x4 (4) - Bytes per input element processed\n0x8 (8) - Right shift for extracting high bits from lookup result  \n0x10 (16) - Left shift for positioning middle lookup value\n0x18 (24) - Left shift for positioning high lookup value\n0x100332e0 - Global pointer to current source buffer position\n0x100332e4 - Global pointer to end source buffer position  \n0x100332e8 - Global pointer to current destination buffer position\n0x100332ec - Global pointer to end destination buffer position\n0x1003674c - Global lookup table base address (g_anLookupTable)\n\nError Handling:\nNo explicit error checking performed\nAssumes valid buffer pointers and sufficient buffer space\nRelies on caller to ensure proper buffer initialization and bounds",
