      "name": "BinkOpen",
      "signature": "int * BinkOpen(char * lpszFilename, uint dwFlags)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "int *",
      "comment": "Opens and initializes a Bink video file for playback.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize local buffer (272 bytes) to zero for Bink instance structure\n2. Record start time using timeGetTime() for performance tracking\n3. Check memory-only flag (0x4000000) to determine data source\n4. If file-based: Initialize BinkFileHandle and read 44-byte header\n5. If memory-based: Copy header data from memory buffer parameter\n6. Validate Bink signature: 'BIKf' (0x664b4942), 'BIKg' (0x674b4942), 'BIKh' (0x684b4942), or 'BIKi' (0x694b4942)\n7. Verify frame count is non-zero to ensure valid compressed data\n8. Extract video dimensions (width, height) and calculate aligned buffer sizes\n9. Set video scaling flags based on dwFlags parameter (2x scaling for certain modes)\n10. Configure frame rate: use global override if BINK_USE_GLOBAL_FRAMERATE (0x1000) set\n11. Calculate memory requirements for video buffers and audio tracks\n12. Allocate main Bink instance structure (0x39c bytes) via AllocateAndSetupMemoryPointers\n13. Copy local buffer data to allocated instance structure\n14. Initialize audio/video buffer pointers based on memory vs file mode\n15. Calculate optimal I/O buffer size using FUN_10008a60\n16. Apply global I/O size override if BINK_USE_GLOBAL_IO_SIZE (0x1000000) set\n17. Configure simulation mode if BINK_SIMULATE_MODE (0x400000) enabled\n18. Set up file reading or memory mapping depending on source type\n19. Initialize playback timing and frame rate conversion\n20. Configure audio track selection and sound system integration\n21. Create background thread for asynchronous frame processing if needed\n22. Return pointer to initialized Bink instance or NULL on failure\n\nParameters:\nlpszFilename (char *): File path to Bink video or memory buffer pointer if memory mode\ndwFlags (uint): Bink playback flags controlling scaling, audio, memory mode, and performance options\n    - 0x4000000: Memory mode (data in lpszFilename parameter)\n    - 0x1000: Use global frame rate override\n    - 0x1000000: Use global I/O buffer size\n    - 0x400000: Enable simulation mode\n    - 0x200000: Disable initial frame preloading\n    - 0x8000000: Skip background thread creation\n    - 0x70000000: Video scaling mode (1x, 2x horizontal, 2x vertical, 2x both)\n    - 0x100000: Enable additional video processing\n    - 0x4000: Audio tracks present flag\n    - 0x2000: Skip decompression buffer allocation\n\nReturns:\nBinkHandle *: Pointer to initialized Bink instance structure for playback operations, or NULL on failure\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Returns NULL if file cannot be opened or invalid Bink signature detected\n- Returns NULL if no compressed frames present in file (frame count = 0)\n- Returns NULL if memory allocation fails during structure initialization\n- Audio track selection uses global g_dwCurrentSoundTrack if available\n- Global frame rate and I/O size overrides reset to 0xFFFFFFFF after use\n- Background thread creation skipped if memory mapping used or explicitly disabled\n\nError Handling:\nAll failures set descriptive error message via BinkSetError() before returning NULL:\n- \"Error opening file.\" - File I/O failure\n- \"Not a Bink file.\" - Invalid signature validation\n- \"The file doesn't contain any compressed frames yet.\" - Zero frame count\n- \"Out of memory.\" - Allocation failure during initialization\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x664b4942: 'BIKf' signature for Bink version F\n0x674b4942: 'BIKg' signature for Bink version G  \n0x684b4942: 'BIKh' signature for Bink version H\n0x694b4942: 'BIKi' signature for Bink version I\n0x39c: Size of main Bink instance structure (924 bytes)\n0x2c: Size of Bink file header (44 bytes)\n0x4000000: Memory mode flag (data source is memory buffer)\n0x70000000: Video scaling mode mask\n0x8000: Bink version H processing flag\n0x18000: Bink version F/G processing flags",
